1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,128
The box is still on the table, and the new phone smells of plastic.

2
00:00:04,178 --> 00:00:07,872
Aziz moves his SIM card into it and installs the mobile bank.

3
00:00:07,922 --> 00:00:11,088
He types his number and his password, and both are right.

4
00:00:11,138 --> 00:00:12,960
Isn't that enough?

5
00:00:13,010 --> 00:00:18,504
A stranger with a swapped SIM card and a guessed password would have exactly the same two things.

6
00:00:18,554 --> 00:00:23,208
In Lesson 23, Aziz logged in on a phone the platform already knew.

7
00:00:23,258 --> 00:00:30,360
This lesson starts on a phone it has never seen: the new-device code, the approval on the old phone, and the account check.

8
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:33,696
The gateway checks the password first, as always.

9
00:00:33,746 --> 00:00:36,072
Nothing starts before a correct password.

10
00:00:36,122 --> 00:00:41,784
Then it looks for the device identifier, and finds nothing: this phone is not known for Aziz.

11
00:00:41,834 --> 00:00:44,784
So the gateway opens a new-device request.

12
00:00:44,834 --> 00:00:48,216
In the master app, the new-device code goes out at once.

13
00:00:48,266 --> 00:00:52,536
TAF sends it by SMS to Aziz's verified number.

14
00:00:52,586 --> 00:00:56,736
The message says it is for a login, and it names the model of the new phone.

15
00:00:56,786 --> 00:00:59,976
The SIM card is in the new phone, so the code lands there.

16
00:01:00,026 --> 00:01:01,848
That proves the SIM.

17
00:01:01,898 --> 00:01:03,912
It does not prove the person.

18
00:01:03,962 --> 00:01:05,784
Now the old phone.

19
00:01:05,834 --> 00:01:09,840
It is still on Aziz's desk, and it still has a session in the mobile bank.

20
00:01:09,890 --> 00:01:13,152
That session passed a green verdict, so it has rank two.

21
00:01:13,202 --> 00:01:15,504
Lesson 29 explains rank.

22
00:01:15,554 --> 00:01:20,664
For now, rank two means a session the platform trusts to answer for Aziz.

23
00:01:20,714 --> 00:01:23,568
The platform sends a push signal to the old phone.

24
00:01:23,618 --> 00:01:27,408
The signal carries no code, only the number of the request.

25
00:01:27,458 --> 00:01:31,224
The old phone then pulls the request over its own session.

26
00:01:31,274 --> 00:01:35,136
It shows the model of the new phone, the network address, and the time.

27
00:01:35,186 --> 00:01:38,904
It shows a warning: never give any code to another person.

28
00:01:38,954 --> 00:01:42,840
And it shows two buttons: Approve, and This was not me.

29
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:46,176
I think the warning matters as much as the buttons.

30
00:01:46,226 --> 00:01:49,464
Many thefts start with a friendly voice that asks for a code.

31
00:01:49,514 --> 00:01:53,976
Here, the old phone never shows a code at all, so there is nothing to read out.

32
00:01:54,026 --> 00:01:58,104
Aziz taps Approve on the old phone, and types the code on the new one.

33
00:01:58,154 --> 00:02:04,344
With a rank-two session on a different phone, the login needs both: a correct code and the approval.

34
00:02:04,394 --> 00:02:09,768
If the old phone stays silent for the whole approval waiting period, a correct code is enough.

35
00:02:09,818 --> 00:02:14,544
If no other phone has a rank-two session, a correct code is enough from the start.

36
00:02:14,594 --> 00:02:18,624
A session of rank one gets only a notice, without the buttons.

37
00:02:18,674 --> 00:02:22,776
Next comes the verdict from TAF, as in Lesson 12.

38
00:02:22,826 --> 00:02:24,648
Today it is green.

39
00:02:24,698 --> 00:02:26,520
Now the account check.

40
00:02:26,570 --> 00:02:30,504
The platform asks one question: what kind of account is this?

41
00:02:30,554 --> 00:02:36,456
If nobody used any of its devices for longer than the dormancy period, the account is dormant.

42
00:02:36,506 --> 00:02:38,664
Lesson 32 covers that.

43
00:02:38,714 --> 00:02:43,536
If the customer was never at level two, the login completes at level one.

44
00:02:43,586 --> 00:02:46,632
When an app needs more, the customer does a step-up.

45
00:02:46,682 --> 00:02:50,616
Aziz was at level two, so his new phone must prove his face.

46
00:02:50,666 --> 00:02:54,624
The mobile bank asks for consent, then runs a MyID re-check.

47
00:02:54,674 --> 00:02:59,544
If the reusable identifier has expired, it runs a full proofing instead.

48
00:02:59,594 --> 00:03:01,416
His face matches.

49
00:03:01,466 --> 00:03:04,200
This is the guard that a stranger cannot pass.

50
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:09,024
The stranger may hold the SIM card, may know the password, and may even catch the code.

51
00:03:09,074 --> 00:03:11,256
But he does not have Aziz's face.

52
00:03:11,306 --> 00:03:13,128
Now the login completes.

53
00:03:13,178 --> 00:03:15,960
The platform makes a record for the new phone.

54
00:03:16,010 --> 00:03:19,440
It passed MyID on this device, so the state is Trusted.

55
00:03:19,490 --> 00:03:21,792
Without a MyID check, it would be Known.

56
00:03:21,842 --> 00:03:23,736
Then comes a new access grant.

57
00:03:23,786 --> 00:03:26,856
Last, the notice: new device login.

58
00:03:26,906 --> 00:03:34,512
It goes by push to Aziz's other devices, and by SMS to his number, with the model, the network address and the time.

59
00:03:34,562 --> 00:03:40,032
The platform writes an audit event, and sends an event to the antifraud system of the bank.

60
00:03:40,082 --> 00:03:41,904
And the other button?

61
00:03:41,954 --> 00:03:47,904
If Aziz had tapped This was not me, the login would end at once, and his number would go on hold.

62
00:03:47,954 --> 00:03:50,112
Lesson 31 shows why.

63
00:03:50,162 --> 00:03:51,984
One last note on side apps.

64
00:03:52,034 --> 00:03:56,712
On a new phone in the marketplace, the approval in the master app replaces the code.

65
00:03:56,762 --> 00:04:03,456
With no rank-two session, the bank's SMS service sends the code, and the session starts at level one.

66
00:04:03,506 --> 00:04:06,408
Lesson 26 covers the details.

67
00:04:06,458 --> 00:04:08,280
To recap.

68
00:04:08,330 --> 00:04:11,928
On a new phone, the number and the password are not enough.

69
00:04:11,978 --> 00:04:13,752
The code proves the SIM card.

70
00:04:13,802 --> 00:04:15,624
The old phone approves.

71
00:04:15,674 --> 00:04:17,688
And MyID proves the face.

72
00:04:17,738 --> 00:04:20,304
Now Aziz is safe from a stranger's hands.

73
00:04:20,354 --> 00:04:26,904
But what if a stranger guides Aziz's own hands, on a phone call, and asks him to install a screen-sharing app?

74
00:04:26,954 --> 00:04:29,040
Next: remote access.
